WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
No, but we don't know it accurately. This is a question that who wrote the Torah? That's important. Now, who wrote the Torah?

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:16.000
Mr. Lariton asked me this question. It's a very important question. We have to know. I mean, we all know that God wrote the Torah, etc.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:22.000
What does it mean specifically? It's a very important thing. My mind, we accept my mind's opinion.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:33.000
That the Torah, from Bereshit to the Enekel Israel, which means the whole Pentateuch, was dictated by God to Moshe Rabbeinu.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:41.000
You see? In other words, God dictates the Torah to Moshe Rabbeinu, and this happens to be one of the cardinal beliefs of Judaism.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:50.000
In other words, this is one of the beliefs that a person cannot, there's no room for mahloket on this particular, you know, another belief there's room for mahloket.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:03.000
On this particular belief, there is no room for mahloket. Namely, that the Torah that we have today, it was written from Bereshit to the Enekel Israel by Moshe Rabbeinu.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:14.000
By the way, we have a ceremony in our community that we perform, yes, and to assert, to declare this belief.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Do you know the ceremony that we perform in the synagogue? No. Every Shabbat.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:28.000
No, we take the Sefer Torah. What do we do? We do not accept Torah.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:38.000
What does it mean? We mean to say specifically that this Sefer Torah, as it is here, is the Sefer Torah that Moses gave to us.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:43.000
It's not written in the Talmud. This ceremony is not written in the Talmud. No place.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:49.000
It's a part of the ceremony that the custom of the Jewish people, and specifically the Sephardic people have the custom, which is a very beautiful custom,

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:55.000
in which we declare that we believe that this Sefer Torah is the Sefer Torah of God.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:00.000
And why do we have to, and why the Sephardic people, they do this? More than all other people. I'll tell you why.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:09.000
The Muslims, they say, that we forged the Torah. Do you know this? Why? Because in the Torah, as it is written,

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:15.000
Ishaq is the son of Sarah, and Ishmael is the son of the concubine.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.000
So they say, this is a forgery. You Jews, you forged the Torah.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.000
So therefore we say, v'zotat Torah, as the psalmist says, in Israel.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.000
I don't know whether it's clear what I'm trying to...

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:29.000
Is it a tradition to follow it through, that it's true?

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.000
Yes, we assert that we believe that this is...

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.000
v'zotat Torah...

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.000
That's the point, you know.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:40.000
So, v'zotat Torah, when he went up to Ashkenazim, right?

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.000
No.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.000
What then?

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.000
Oh, that's important. How did he get it, and when did he get it?

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:49.000
Now, and this, there's already more than one opinion.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:55.000
The opinion that I accept, that is my mind's opinion, is the following.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:58.000
He got messages of the Torah.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:11.000
By the way, Moshe Rabbeinu, at that time, would write, or would teach the people, orally, what the Torah is.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:15.000
No, but at that time, during the 40 years,

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.000
the 40 years of the desert, when there were, you know,

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.000
there was no sefer Torah yet.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.000
The sefer Torah was right before his death.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.000
Before his death, God told him,

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:32.000
now, from Bereshit to Lehanikon Israel, I'm going to give you the whole Torah.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.000
And right before his death,

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.000
he gave him the sefer Torah, Moshe Rabbeinu.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:42.000
In other words, the 40 years of the desert, there was no sefer Torah.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.000
There was only...

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.000
Where does it say that, actually?

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.000
Where does it say this in the Torah?

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:49.000
That's a tradition.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:51.000
And this is more than...

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:54.000
I said there's more than one opinion on this.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.000
My opinion of Rambam in Sahadiya Gaon is that

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:02.000
this was the Torah, the sefer Torah, was given only Litmim Oto.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.000
You know, there was a sefer Torah.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:06.000
It was given by God to him, and he wrote it.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:07.000
Yes.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.000
It was the same person.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.000
And God, because Moshe is not the author.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.000
God is the author.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.000
What happened in the 40 years?

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.000
Oh, so that is the point of this.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.000
During the 40 years,

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:22.000
there was no Torah shevichtav.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:25.000
It was only Torah shevichtave, oral tradition.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:28.000
And from here, the rabbis, like Rambam,

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:31.000
he said, from here we learn

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:34.000
that oral tradition, according to Jewish law,

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:38.000
has precedence over written law.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:44.000
Because the Torah was known before orally,

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.000
and after it was put into writing.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:50.000
Again, because the Christians say,

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:52.000
and the Karaites and other people say,

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:55.000
well, yes, we accept the Pentateuch,

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:58.000
yes, or the Torah, but we interpret it our own way.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:01.000
So we say, no, you can't interpret the Torah your own way.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:04.000
You have to interpret your Torah according to the tradition.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:06.000
They say, well, what do we mean tradition?

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.000
We don't want to accept tradition.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:11.000
So we say, no, well, tradition has precedence

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:13.000
over the Torah.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:16.000
The Torah first was known as Torah shevichtav.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.000
The Torah was known orally.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.000
For instance, like Saadia Gaon,

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.000
there is a book that he wrote,

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.000
it's still in Arabic, nobody translated it.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:28.000
It's Kitab al-Maraqib.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.000
Kitab al-Anwar al-Maraqib.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.000
And it's a very interesting book.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.000
And then, in this book, he says,

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.000
he says like this.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:38.000
It's obviously that what is binding,

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:40.000
according to Jewish tradition,

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.000
the most binding thing is the oral law.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:44.000
God spoke the Ten Commandments.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:46.000
He says the Ten Commandments.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:49.000
Then Moshe Rabbeinu went up to heaven to bring the tablets.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:52.000
In between, the Jewish people commit idolatry.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:54.000
You remember?

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:56.000
And God is very mad.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.000
So he said, well, there was no Torah given to them.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:00.000
It was only oral law.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.000
Torah shevichtav was.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:04.000
God only spoke to them. He didn't give them a document,

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:06.000
a written document.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.000
So what you see from here,

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.000
that the most important thing of the Torah

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.000
is not the actual written document,

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.000
but the Torah that was spoken.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:16.000
What was the oral law?

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:18.000
No, there was more than that.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.000
The oral law, this is a question that you're asking,

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:22.000
a very important question.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:24.000
This is very important, what you're asking.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:26.000
It's a very basic question.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:28.000
We always say, we believe in Torah shevichtav.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:30.000
What is it?

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:32.000
This should be explained.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:34.000
Maybe one day, you know,

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:36.000
after we study a little bit more of Umayyad,

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:38.000
maybe we should switch

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:40.000
and try to speak about the basis

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.000
of the Torah.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:44.000
What Torah shevichtav, what Torah shevichav,

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:46.000
what Adam and Eve, what they did, what they do,

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:48.000
how a law is derived,

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:50.000
all those things are very important questions.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:52.000
I have to study it a lot,

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:54.000
but before this I want to answer a point

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:56.000
that he said.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:58.000
According to us, what we believe is

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:00.000
that the word of the Torah, any written word,

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.000
could be twisted

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:04.000
any way you want it.

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:06.000
Anyway, there is no word, no matter how clear

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:08.000
it is written, that it cannot be twisted.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:10.000
Let me give you an example.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:12.000
This example is made by my mind.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:14.000
Take the most important passage of the Torah.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:16.000
Shema Yisrael, Adonai Eloheinu, Adonai Echad.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:18.000
What does it mean, Shema Yisrael, Adonai Eloheinu, Adonai Echad?

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:20.000
Listen, there is one God.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:22.000
If you want to twist it, you could twist it.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:24.000
You say, Shema Yisrael,

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.000
Adonai, one.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:28.000
Eloheinu, two.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:30.000
Adonai, three.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.000
These three are Echad,

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:34.000
the Trinity of the Catholics.

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:36.000
You could twist it.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:38.000
There is no passage where you cannot twist it.

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:40.000
So finally, in the final answer,

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:42.000
in the final analysis, when we say it,

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:44.000
our tradition says

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:46.000
that we must interpret

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:48.000
the passage in the way we say it.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:50.000
In other words, we must always

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:52.000
return, go back,

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.000
to tradition.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:56.000
Because otherwise, you know, the Torah,

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:58.000
says,

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.000
How do you know where this definition is?

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:04.000
How do you know?

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:08.000
In the seventh day,

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:10.000
in the eighth day, he should

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:12.000
cut his flesh.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.000
What flesh?

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:16.000
Maybe the flesh here.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:18.000
How do you know it's a tradition?

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.000
It's a tradition that tells you this.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:24.000
So therefore, for us,

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:26.000
it is immensely important, our tradition.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:28.000
If you believe in tradition,

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:30.000
then you must believe in family.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.000
You cannot believe in tradition.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:34.000
Why? How do you know it's tradition?

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:36.000
My father told me.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:38.000
So that's why,

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:40.000
when there is no family alive,

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:42.000
no Judaism.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:46.000
I was talking to some youngsters,

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:48.000
young American Jews,

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:50.000
and I said, still tradition is important.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:52.000
I said to youngsters who were completely not,

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:54.000
you know, far from religious.

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:56.000
Far, far.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:58.000
So I said, and basically, we all are Jews.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:00.000
Most of us are Jews because of tradition.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:02.000
I said, how do you know that you're Jewish?

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:04.000
Basically because your father told you so.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:06.000
But he's not a Jew unless his mother is a Jew.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:10.000
But how do you know that you're Jewish?

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:12.000
Every one of you.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:14.000
Because your father told you so.

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:16.000
And you were raised, your father told you,

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:18.000
I want you to be Jewish.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:20.000
Or whatever it is.

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:22.000
Even if you don't go to Hebrew school,

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:24.000
how do you know that you're Jewish?

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:26.000
Because of tradition.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:28.000
My father told me I'm Jewish.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:30.000
If you don't accept that,

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.000
we have to go into the homage.

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:34.000
No.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:36.000
Because that question, I'm sure, is good,

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:38.000
and will take me more than a few minutes to answer it.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:40.000
I know, it's not just a yes or no.

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:42.000
You people of ours, you know that it's a German name.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:44.000
We are German.

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:46.000
Any name, you know,

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:48.000
any name could be a good name.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:50.000
And, you know, we have no proof.

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:52.000
We have none. Wait a minute.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:54.000
There's people in the name of Cohen, in the name of Jews, and all that.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:56.000
I don't believe you.

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:58.000
It's just a yes or no.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:00.000
Yes.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:06.000
To sum up what you said,

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:08.000
the Torah doesn't exist without the Gemara, then?

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:10.000
Of course!

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:12.000
The Torah has no meaning without

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:14.000
Torah shevichtav has no meaning without Torah shevichtav.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:16.000
Why?

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:18.000
The Catholics accept Torah shevichtav,

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.000
but they twist it all

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:22.000
for their own nonsense.

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:24.000
What's the difference between us and Catholicism?

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:26.000
It's only Torah shevichtav.

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:30.000
You know?

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:32.000
I beg your pardon?

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:34.000
But you're saying that Moshe is the one who wrote the Torah down from God?

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:36.000
From God. He was a scribe, you know.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:38.000
But isn't it so that Torah shevichtav

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:40.000
is also a derivative of the same scribe?

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:42.000
Yes, but I can explain one day.

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:44.000
Why did you write it down then?

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:46.000
How about tonight?

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:50.000
Gentlemen.

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:52.000
Gentlemen.

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:54.000
I will begin with the second chapter.

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:56.000
Now,

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:00.000
the next verses

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:02.000
are very difficult verses.

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:04.000
We read them at least once a week

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:06.000
in Shabbat.

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:08.000
But it still requires some sort of explanation.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:10.000
Alright?

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:12.000
And thus,

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:16.000
as it was described in the preceding chapter,

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:18.000
they were finished.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:20.000
But in Hebrew, vaykulu,

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:22.000
it comes from the word called.

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:24.000
It means they were completed.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:26.000
They were brought into perfection.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:28.000
You know, to the ultimate perfection.

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:30.000
Vaykulu comes from the word called. Called means the maximum.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:32.000
Yes?

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:34.000
And God created a maximum world.

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:36.000
The most perfect world. Vaykulu.

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:38.000
Vaykulu is a very important word.

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:40.000
Vaykulu ha-shamayim v'ha-arez.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:42.000
It doesn't mean that they just weren't finished.

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:44.000
That means they were brought to their perfection,

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:46.000
to their outmost limit.

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:48.000
Ha-shamayim v'ha-arez.

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:50.000
And by shamayim v'ha-arez, we don't mean

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:52.000
something abstract, but rather

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:54.000
because of everything that is included

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:56.000
in both shamayim v'ha-arez.

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:58.000
Vaykulu ha-shamayim v'ha-arez,

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:00.000
v'chol se-va-am.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:02.000
Now,

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:04.000
vaykul elohim vayom

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:06.000
ha-shemeim, and God

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:08.000
brought into perfection

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:10.000
the same word from kalal, from kol.

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:12.000
Yes?

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:14.000
In the seventh day, menachto ha-shahasa,

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:16.000
the work that he had done

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:18.000
previously.

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:20.000
This pasuk is a very difficult pasuk.

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:22.000
Very, very difficult.

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:24.000
And I'm going to bring the difficulty

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:26.000
to your attention. I don't know how to solve it well,

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:28.000
but nonetheless you should know the difficulty.

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:30.000
Here it says that

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:32.000
God finished his work which day?

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:34.000
Not Shabbat.

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:36.000
In Shabbat, not in the Friday.

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:38.000
He finished it

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:40.000
when?

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:42.000
Ha-shemeim?

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:44.000
She said,

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:46.000
vaykul elohim vayom ha-shishi

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:48.000
menachto ha-shahasa.

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:50.000
Why did he say vayom ha-shishi?

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:52.000
He checked it.

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:54.000
Two answers which I know

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:56.000
and

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:58.000
you pick which one

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:00.000
seems more logical to you.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.000
One answer is

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:04.000
vaykul means, vayom ha-shemeim means

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:06.000
up, you know, before

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:08.000
vayom ha-shemeim. Vaykul elohim vayom ha-shemeim

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:10.000
means vaykul

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:12.000
at the moment of the beginning

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:14.000
of vayom ha-shemeim. God knows exactly

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:16.000
the second, at that particular second

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:18.000
God finished.

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:20.000
Vayom ha-shemeim means at the beginning

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:22.000
of vayom ha-shemeim.

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:24.000
That is one explanation.

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:26.000
In other words, we have to wait

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:28.000
10 or 15 minutes or whatever it is before

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:30.000
we have to stay where? But God

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:32.000
vayom ha-shemeim in that particular instant

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:34.000
he finished.

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:36.000
That is one explanation.

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:38.000
Second explanation

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:40.000
is a more mystical explanation.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:42.000
I'm going to Kabbalah.

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:44.000
But I bring it to you because

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:46.000
it makes a little bit of sense

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:48.000
and common sense.

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:50.000
So I'm going to say this.

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:52.000
God, they

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:54.000
read this pasuk as follows.

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:56.000
Vaykul elohim.

00:13:56.000 --> 00:13:58.000
God finished. Vayom ha-shemeim

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:00.000
by the fact that God created

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:02.000
a Shabbat.

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:04.000
That creation

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:06.000
of Shabbat is the conclusion

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:08.000
of all his work.

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:10.000
It's part of his creation.

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:12.000
You understand? Let me read this for you

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:14.000
again. Vaykul elohim

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:16.000
vayom ha-shemeim.

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:18.000
Melach torah shehasa.

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:20.000
By creating

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:22.000
the yom ha-shemeim, God finished

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:24.000
all his work.

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:26.000
Vaykul elohim

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:28.000
by yom ha-shemeim.

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:30.000
Yes?

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:32.000
By making the yom ha-shemeim, God finished

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:34.000
kol melach torah shehasa.

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:36.000
You understand? In other words, that the yom Shabbat

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:38.000
is part of the original

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:40.000
creation and that yom Shabbat

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:42.000
is the crowning completion

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:44.000
of the whole

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:46.000
creation.

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:48.000
So there's two explanations. The first explanation is

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:50.000
peshad.

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:52.000
And the second is a little bit more

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:54.000
deeper. You could extract

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:56.000
either or both or perhaps

00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:58.000
none if you have your own.

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:00.000
You can't deny that the seventh day itself is a creation.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:02.000
That's right.

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:04.000
Therefore, there has to have been

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:06.000
even though...

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:08.000
He created the yom ha-shemeim.

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:10.000
This is one explanation which is

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:12.000
to Kabbalah.

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:14.000
And I think it makes

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:16.000
a little bit of sense.

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:18.000
But this happened to be written by

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:20.000
the Hakim of Kabbalah.

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:22.000
Because you can't take away that the seventh day was created.

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:24.000
Yes, but...

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:26.000
Yes, go on.

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:28.000
Does yom ha-shemeim start from

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:30.000
like Friday night sundown?

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:32.000
Friday night sundown, yes.

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:34.000
Now,

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:36.000
vayishvot by yom ha-shemeim

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:38.000
kol melach torah shehasa.

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:40.000
And thus, in the seventh day,

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:42.000
God rested. What does it mean rested

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:44.000
in Hebrew? Vayishvot in Hebrew

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:46.000
doesn't mean rested like in English.

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:48.000
And that's important to understand.

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:50.000
Vayishvot in English to rest

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:52.000
means that you're tired.

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:54.000
And rest is a positive word

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:56.000
in our words. And you rest.

00:15:56.000 --> 00:15:58.000
In Hebrew, vayishvot is a negative

00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:00.000
word. It means to abstain of doing

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.000
something. So vayishvot

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:04.000
by yom ha-shemeim means

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:06.000
God abstained in the seventh day

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:08.000
kol melach torah shehasa of doing

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:10.000
any sort of work.

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:12.000
In other words, it doesn't mean that God

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:14.000
rested on the seventh day, but rather

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:16.000
that God abstained

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:18.000
from doing any particular

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:20.000
action or creation.

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:22.000
What would be the difference?

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:24.000
Oh, the difference is

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:26.000
a norm.

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:28.000
Rested presupposes

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:30.000
that somebody is tired

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:32.000
and therefore physical exhaustion

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:34.000
and then

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:36.000
you rest. But in vayishvot

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:38.000
doesn't mean to rest in that sense.

00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:40.000
In Hebrew, vayishvot means to abstain

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:42.000
abstain from doing

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:44.000
something.

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:46.000
Shabbat means you abstain from doing

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:48.000
things.

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:50.000
Now comes the next word.

00:16:50.000 --> 00:16:52.000
So now,

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:54.000
vayishvot by yom ha-shemeim

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:56.000
kol melach torah shehasa.

00:16:56.000 --> 00:16:58.000
God abstained from doing things.

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:00.000
By the blood of Elohim in yom ha-shemeim.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:02.000
And God made a special

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:04.000
blessing for the yom ha-shemeim.

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:06.000
By katesh yotor. And God

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:08.000
declared it to be kadosh.

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:10.000
What does it mean in Hebrew, kadosh?

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:12.000
Separate.

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:14.000
Separate. Different.

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:16.000
You see? When we say to God, atah kadosh

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:18.000
what do we mean?

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:20.000
Atah kadosh.

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:22.000
We say that

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:24.000
God, you are different.

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:26.000
Because we cannot describe God with positive terms.

00:17:26.000 --> 00:17:28.000
How could you describe God?

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:30.000
Could you say to God, you are good?

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:32.000
The word good for you means something

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:34.000
that is basically human.

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:36.000
You say somebody is a good man

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:38.000
means that he behaves

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:40.000
because he has an interest.

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:42.000
But that transcends goodness.

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:44.000
How could you describe God?

00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:46.000
The best description of God is kadosh.

00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:48.000
Kadosh means

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:50.000
you are different than anything we know.

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:52.000
That's why we say kadosh, kadosh, kadosh.

00:17:52.000 --> 00:17:54.000
You know why we say

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:56.000
three times kadosh?

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:58.000
Of course.

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:00.000
Because there is, you see,

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:02.000
God is different.

00:18:02.000 --> 00:18:04.000
There are three types of creation.

00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:06.000
There is

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:08.000
the earth

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:10.000
which is material

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:12.000
and it has a beginning

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:14.000
and also could be destroyed by God.

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:16.000
That's one thing. It's material

00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:18.000
and it has

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:20.000
an end.

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:22.000
Then we have

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:24.000
human beings who are

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:26.000
partially

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:28.000
spiritual,

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:30.000
but we have a beginning

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:32.000
and we have an end.

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:34.000
Then there is the malakhim

00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:36.000
who are spiritual, they have no end,

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:38.000
they will never be destroyed, but they have a beginning.

00:18:38.000 --> 00:18:40.000
So therefore we say kadosh.

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:42.000
First being kadosh, you are different

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:44.000
than this material world.

00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:48.000
Why? Because God is neither material

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:50.000
nor has a beginning nor has an end.

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:52.000
Then kadosh,

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:54.000
you are also different than the

00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:56.000
intermediate world, the human world,

00:18:56.000 --> 00:18:58.000
in the sense that, because, all right,

00:18:58.000 --> 00:19:00.000
you are not material, and also

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:02.000
we have an end.

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:04.000
God has no end.

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:06.000
Also kadosh, kadosh, kadosh,

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:08.000
also the malakhim, the highest malakhim,

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:10.000
also they say to God

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:12.000
kadosh. Why? Because although they are

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:14.000
immortal, they will never perish,

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:16.000
and they are spiritual, still they

00:19:16.000 --> 00:19:18.000
are created, and God is not

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:20.000
created. So each one,

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:22.000
so that's why the Gemara says in one place

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:24.000
that there are three worlds.

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:26.000
The lower world says kadosh, kadosh, kadosh,

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:28.000
there is another world that says kadosh, kadosh,

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:30.000
and the highest malakhim will say only

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:32.000
once kadosh, because the difference

00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:34.000
between them and Borea Olam is basically

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:36.000
one, in the sense that Borea

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:38.000
Olam is

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:40.000
a creator, and they are created.

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:42.000
So why do we say it three times?

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:44.000
We say it like...

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:46.000
The three different things?

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:48.000
I tell you why, because we have,

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:50.000
it's a good question,

00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:52.000
because we have the three worlds together.

00:19:52.000 --> 00:19:54.000
Part of us

00:19:54.000 --> 00:19:56.000
is like the malakhim,

00:19:56.000 --> 00:19:58.000
part of us

00:19:58.000 --> 00:20:00.000
is like the

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:02.000
material, and part of us is like the

00:20:02.000 --> 00:20:04.000
secondary world. That's true, we have the three worlds,

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:06.000
we have it in us. Maybe

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:08.000
that's the reason, I don't know.

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:10.000
So now, vaikadosh

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:12.000
elohim et yom ha'shevi,

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:14.000
vaikadosh otom. And why

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:16.000
kadosh otom?

00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:18.000
Because in it

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:20.000
he stopped from doing any kind

00:20:20.000 --> 00:20:22.000
of work. I said bara elohim la'asot.

00:20:22.000 --> 00:20:24.000
What does it mean to say bara elohim la'asot?

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:26.000
Two explanations. One, which I

00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:28.000
consider peshat, and the other I consider adilash.

00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:30.000
Bara elohim la'asot is

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:32.000
God created this world.

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:34.000
But this world, bara elohim

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:36.000
la'asot, will always continue

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:38.000
to reproduce whatever God did.

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:40.000
In other words, bara

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:42.000
elohim, and

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:44.000
bara otom la'asot, that this

00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:46.000
world always will continue repeating

00:20:46.000 --> 00:20:48.000
the same things that God made to it.

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:50.000
Now it's the laws that God

00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:52.000
made into the

00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:54.000
universe, that will be la'asot,

00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:56.000
always will be continued, perpetual,

00:20:56.000 --> 00:20:58.000
to go over and over and over.

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:00.000
That is the simple explanation

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:02.000
I said, bara elohim

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:04.000
la'asot, to make itself

00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:06.000
over and over to repeat itself.

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:08.000
That's one explanation which I consider peshat. Another

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:10.000
explanation which is nice, I said, bara elohim.

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:12.000
But now it's up to man

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:14.000
la'asot.

00:21:14.000 --> 00:21:16.000
God created the universe, but

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:18.000
we have to act in this

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:20.000
universe. I don't know whether it's clear

00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:22.000
that explanation or not.

00:21:22.000 --> 00:21:24.000
Now, before,

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:26.000
we have to do something also.

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:28.000
Since,

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:44.000
since Shabbat is such an important

00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:46.000
mitzvah, and

00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:48.000
since the explanation of Shabbat

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:50.000
is written in this yufay-sukrim,

00:21:50.000 --> 00:21:52.000
and since we read this

00:21:52.000 --> 00:21:54.000
every time,

00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:56.000
at least once a week, I think

00:21:56.000 --> 00:21:58.000
that it's proper for us to

00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:00.000
try to understand what does it say here.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:02.000
It's something very, very deep.

00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:04.000
It says like this. Hold it, Dennis.

00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:16.000
Gentlemen.

00:22:18.000 --> 00:22:20.000
I said, bara elohim la'asot is a very important pasuk.

00:22:20.000 --> 00:22:22.000
I'll tell you why. What does it say here?

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:24.000
Here it says

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:26.000
that God created

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:28.000
the world in six days,

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:30.000
stopped from doing work

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:32.000
at the seventh, and that's why He made the

00:22:32.000 --> 00:22:34.000
young Shabbat. What does it mean? It means like this.

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:36.000
Something very, very important.

00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:38.000
It means that when we

00:22:38.000 --> 00:22:40.000
observe the Shabbat,

00:22:40.000 --> 00:22:42.000
we assert,

00:22:42.000 --> 00:22:44.000
we declare that we, in point of fact,

00:22:44.000 --> 00:22:46.000
we believe that God created the world,

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:48.000
and He stopped in

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:50.000
the seventh day. It means like this.

00:22:50.000 --> 00:22:52.000
First of all, God created

00:22:52.000 --> 00:22:54.000
the world. What does it mean that He created the world?

00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:56.000
It means something very simple. That at one time

00:22:56.000 --> 00:22:58.000
the world was not created,

00:22:58.000 --> 00:23:00.000
and just out of the blue,

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:02.000
God decided to create the world.

00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:04.000
Which means God was not forced

00:23:04.000 --> 00:23:06.000
by anyone to create

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:08.000
the world. At one time there was no world,

00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:10.000
and God decided

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:12.000
to do the work.

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:14.000
And the same God who decided to do the work,

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:16.000
He decided to stop working

00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:18.000
at the seventh day.

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:20.000
Which means that God is absolutely

00:23:20.000 --> 00:23:22.000
omnipotent. He does whatever

00:23:22.000 --> 00:23:24.000
He wants to.

00:23:24.000 --> 00:23:26.000
Now, we call, how do we call

00:23:26.000 --> 00:23:28.000
Shabbat in the Torah?

00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:30.000
Shabbat is an oath.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:32.000
Oath Healer Olam.

00:23:32.000 --> 00:23:34.000
It is a sign. What does it mean that

00:23:34.000 --> 00:23:36.000
the Shabbat is a sign?

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:38.000
Let me ask you a question.

00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:40.000
Could you, in your own mind,

00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:42.000
prove to me

00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:44.000
that God created

00:23:44.000 --> 00:23:46.000
the world in six days,

00:23:46.000 --> 00:23:48.000
and He stopped in the seven days? Could you ever prove this?

00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:50.000
It's impossible to prove.

00:23:50.000 --> 00:23:52.000
Creation,

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:54.000
something from nothing, is so difficult

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:56.000
that no human mind

00:23:56.000 --> 00:23:58.000
could ever prove it one way or the other.

00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:00.000
You cannot prove.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:02.000
Well, certainly that you cannot prove.

00:24:02.000 --> 00:24:04.000
It's impossible to prove.

00:24:04.000 --> 00:24:06.000
So therefore, how could we know

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:08.000
that God, in point of fact,

00:24:08.000 --> 00:24:10.000
stopped working

00:24:10.000 --> 00:24:12.000
in the seven days?

00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:14.000
We don't know. It's an act of faith.

00:24:14.000 --> 00:24:16.000
God is telling us, if you believe in Me,

00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:18.000
if you believe that what I'm

00:24:18.000 --> 00:24:20.000
saying to you is true, stop working

00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:22.000
on Shabbat.

00:24:22.000 --> 00:24:24.000
It's like

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:26.000
you say to your wife, she tells you

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:28.000
sometimes, if you love Me, you do this to Me.

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:30.000
The same way.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:32.000
Well,

00:24:32.000 --> 00:24:34.000
it's a conversion issue because it's

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:36.000
a psychological act.

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:38.000
If you believe in Him,

00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:40.000
you do this. Because you cannot understand it.

00:24:40.000 --> 00:24:42.000
Somebody sometimes says,

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:44.000
what would happen if I understand that the world was created?

00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:46.000
You can never prove it.

00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:48.000
It's only an act of faith.

00:24:48.000 --> 00:24:50.000
And because it is an act of faith,

00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:52.000
God says, well, if you actually,

00:24:52.000 --> 00:24:54.000
in point of fact, you believe that

00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:56.000
I am omnipotent, I am all-powerful,

00:24:56.000 --> 00:24:58.000
etc., etc.,

00:24:58.000 --> 00:25:00.000
you stop working on Shabbat. And there is no

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:02.000
excuse for Shabbat.

00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:04.000
If you believe that God is omnipotent,

00:25:04.000 --> 00:25:06.000
then there is no room for any kind of recourse.

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:08.000
So that's the whole idea

00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:10.000
of Shabbat as it is brought here.

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:12.000
In other words, we believe in Shabbat

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:14.000
by fulfilling the Shabbat

00:25:14.000 --> 00:25:16.000
we declare, not with words,

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:18.000
but with concrete

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:20.000
actions that, you know,

00:25:20.000 --> 00:25:22.000
once I had to speak to ladies,

00:25:22.000 --> 00:25:24.000
a group of ladies, and I had to explain the Shabbat.

00:25:24.000 --> 00:25:26.000
And I was telling them

00:25:26.000 --> 00:25:28.000
this explanation, more or less.

00:25:28.000 --> 00:25:30.000
They said, well, what happens if I believe in my own

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:32.000
mind, I believe it,

00:25:32.000 --> 00:25:34.000
but, you know, I'm not observing Shabbat.

00:25:34.000 --> 00:25:36.000
So I said,

00:25:36.000 --> 00:25:38.000
there were ladies, I said, you know,

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:40.000
there's two kinds of love. There's the Jewish

00:25:40.000 --> 00:25:42.000
concept of love, and there's the Goic concept of love.

00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:44.000
Jewish concept of love is love has to be

00:25:44.000 --> 00:25:46.000
expressed in concrete actions.

00:25:46.000 --> 00:25:48.000
The Goic concept of love is love is mainly

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:50.000
what you believe, what you feel, and you don't have to

00:25:50.000 --> 00:25:52.000
show it in your actions. Let's say, for instance,

00:25:52.000 --> 00:25:54.000
say, you marry.

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:56.000
Suppose, you know, there's a husband, say, the husband

00:25:56.000 --> 00:25:58.000
loves his wife. There's a kind of husband

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:00.000
who will fool around when his wife

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:02.000
doesn't know.

00:26:02.000 --> 00:26:04.000
And then there's another

00:26:04.000 --> 00:26:06.000
who says, no, I love her, and I'm not,

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:08.000
I want to act accordingly.

00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:10.000
So I said, the Jewish concept of Shabbat

00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:12.000
is that love

00:26:12.000 --> 00:26:14.000
for God has to be expressed

00:26:14.000 --> 00:26:16.000
in concrete actions.

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:18.000
There cannot be expressions with words.

00:26:18.000 --> 00:26:20.000
You have to fulfill and behave

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:22.000
accordingly. And I said, you ladies,

00:26:22.000 --> 00:26:24.000
you, for instance, you know,

00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:26.000
nobody wants that, you know, you want

00:26:26.000 --> 00:26:28.000
demands from your husband. What kind of love do you demand

00:26:28.000 --> 00:26:30.000
from your husband? A concrete

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:32.000
love, concrete actions.

00:26:32.000 --> 00:26:34.000
You see, same thing for your woman,

00:26:34.000 --> 00:26:36.000
demands from her husband, you know.

00:26:36.000 --> 00:26:38.000
Not an abstract, theoretic love, but a concrete

00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:40.000
action. Because I think that this

00:26:40.000 --> 00:26:42.000
expression is a valid expression. Nonetheless,

00:26:42.000 --> 00:26:44.000
we want to continue further.

00:26:44.000 --> 00:26:46.000
It says,

00:26:50.000 --> 00:26:52.000
Let me tell you the translation,

00:26:52.000 --> 00:26:54.000
and, um,

00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:56.000
and then I will tell you

00:26:56.000 --> 00:26:58.000
there are several explanations to this translation.

00:26:58.000 --> 00:27:00.000
These are the generations

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:02.000
of the heavens and earth

00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:04.000
at the time of their creation.

00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:06.000
There are

00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:08.000
two basic explanations.

00:27:08.000 --> 00:27:10.000
One explanation means, Ele refers to the above

00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:12.000
mentions. It means, in the above mentioned

00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:14.000
way, the world was

00:27:14.000 --> 00:27:16.000
created.

00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:18.000
It means, in this way,

00:27:18.000 --> 00:27:20.000
the Shammai and the Ares, they came to be.

00:27:20.000 --> 00:27:22.000
Toledo means the generations. In this way,

00:27:22.000 --> 00:27:24.000
the Shammai and the Ares were

00:27:24.000 --> 00:27:26.000
created.

00:27:26.000 --> 00:27:28.000
Ele means, in the way that

00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:30.000
we described in the preceding chapter.

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:32.000
All right?

00:27:32.000 --> 00:27:34.000
This is one. There's another

00:27:34.000 --> 00:27:36.000
expression which I prefer, and it is,

00:27:38.000 --> 00:27:40.000
Let's see the future,

00:27:40.000 --> 00:27:42.000
next chapter,

00:27:42.000 --> 00:27:44.000
this chapter will tell you what happened

00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:46.000
to Shammai and the Ares

00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:48.000
after the creation.

00:27:48.000 --> 00:27:50.000
So, the two explanations,

00:27:50.000 --> 00:27:52.000
which one,

00:27:52.000 --> 00:27:54.000
either way you want it.

00:27:54.000 --> 00:27:56.000
One,

00:27:56.000 --> 00:27:58.000
Ele,

00:27:58.000 --> 00:28:00.000
refers to the above

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:02.000
chapter.

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:04.000
In the above mentioned way,

00:28:04.000 --> 00:28:06.000
Shammai and the Ares came

00:28:06.000 --> 00:28:08.000
to be.

00:28:08.000 --> 00:28:10.000
Adra says no.

00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:12.000
Ele refers to the next chapter,

00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:14.000
to chapter two.

00:28:14.000 --> 00:28:16.000
You're going to see what happened to Shammai and the Ares.

00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:18.000
And what you're going to see, according to Adra,

00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:20.000
is that Adam sinned,

00:28:20.000 --> 00:28:22.000
because Adam changed, there was a

00:28:22.000 --> 00:28:24.000
change in Shammai and the Ares.

00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:26.000
Now, with the Shammai and the Ares,

00:28:26.000 --> 00:28:28.000
the world changed.

00:28:28.000 --> 00:28:30.000
And why is it important to say this?

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:32.000
Because, if you remember in

00:28:32.000 --> 00:28:34.000
Basuk 31,

00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:36.000
of the preceding chapter,

00:28:36.000 --> 00:28:38.000
says,

00:28:38.000 --> 00:28:40.000
that God saw everything

00:28:40.000 --> 00:28:42.000
that it was created,

00:28:42.000 --> 00:28:44.000
and it was very good.

00:28:44.000 --> 00:28:46.000
So, if everything that

00:28:46.000 --> 00:28:48.000
is created is very good,

00:28:48.000 --> 00:28:50.000
how come that we have so much trouble in the world?

00:28:51.000 --> 00:28:53.000
How come we have so many

00:28:53.000 --> 00:28:55.000
problems?

00:28:55.000 --> 00:28:57.000
Oh, so that's why,

00:28:57.000 --> 00:28:59.000
that's even Adra's explanation.

00:28:59.000 --> 00:29:01.000
That's why it's telling you now that Basuk,

00:29:01.000 --> 00:29:03.000
Ele, Tole, Lota, Shammai and the Ares,

00:29:03.000 --> 00:29:05.000
now you're going to see what happened.

00:29:05.000 --> 00:29:07.000
At one time, there was top men all.

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:09.000
But then, men changed

00:29:09.000 --> 00:29:11.000
this top men all.

00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:13.000
And therefore,

00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:15.000
the function of the

00:29:15.000 --> 00:29:17.000
second chapter, of this chapter, is to teach you

00:29:17.000 --> 00:29:19.000
how come that at one time

00:29:19.000 --> 00:29:21.000
was top men all, and right now

00:29:21.000 --> 00:29:23.000
we don't find that top men all.

00:29:23.000 --> 00:29:25.000
Do you, gentlemen?

00:29:34.000 --> 00:29:36.000
There are many, many, but

00:29:36.000 --> 00:29:38.000
nobody knows the actual...

00:29:38.000 --> 00:29:40.000
I wrote an article,

00:29:40.000 --> 00:29:42.000
by the way, explaining

00:29:42.000 --> 00:29:44.000
all, you know,

00:29:44.000 --> 00:29:46.000
all the places in the Torah, in the whole Torah,

00:29:46.000 --> 00:29:48.000
where there is an

00:29:49.000 --> 00:29:51.000
and I mentioned it,

00:29:51.000 --> 00:29:53.000
and I explained it, but, you know,

00:29:53.000 --> 00:29:55.000
I don't take it seriously, even when I wrote it.

00:29:57.000 --> 00:29:59.000
You don't take seriously what he wrote?

00:29:59.000 --> 00:30:01.000
Yes, because it's all there.

00:30:03.000 --> 00:30:05.000
The author said, if it goes up, then it goes down.

00:30:07.000 --> 00:30:09.000
That's an explanation, then,

00:30:09.000 --> 00:30:11.000
that

00:30:11.000 --> 00:30:13.000
evil would be the absence

00:30:13.000 --> 00:30:15.000
of the good. No, no, we're going to see.

00:30:15.000 --> 00:30:17.000
Gentlemen, please.

00:30:18.000 --> 00:30:20.000
Let's see.

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:26.000
Let's take it like it can be a problem.

00:30:26.000 --> 00:30:28.000
This following are the

00:30:28.000 --> 00:30:30.000
consequences of the aftermath

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:32.000
of what happened to the world,

00:30:32.000 --> 00:30:34.000
and, you know, to Shammai Ba'al

00:30:34.000 --> 00:30:36.000
after the creation,

00:30:38.000 --> 00:30:40.000
Now,

00:30:40.000 --> 00:30:42.000
at that moment,

00:30:44.000 --> 00:30:46.000
all the font

00:30:47.000 --> 00:30:49.000
should be explained as

00:30:49.000 --> 00:30:51.000
font, you know,

00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:53.000
trees which are

00:30:53.000 --> 00:30:55.000
of no use,

00:30:55.000 --> 00:30:57.000
like fonts.

00:30:57.000 --> 00:30:59.000
Because

00:30:59.000 --> 00:31:01.000
there was not

00:31:01.000 --> 00:31:03.000
in the land.

00:31:03.000 --> 00:31:05.000
At that moment, when God created

00:31:05.000 --> 00:31:07.000
all, every vegetation was

00:31:07.000 --> 00:31:09.000
a useful vegetation.

00:31:09.000 --> 00:31:11.000
No weeds.

00:31:11.000 --> 00:31:13.000
Because

00:31:13.000 --> 00:31:15.000
because

00:31:15.000 --> 00:31:17.000
and all the weeds,

00:31:17.000 --> 00:31:19.000
there was no

00:31:19.000 --> 00:31:21.000
weeds and no

00:31:21.000 --> 00:31:23.000
thorns in the land. There was

00:31:23.000 --> 00:31:25.000
vegetation, but all vegetation

00:31:25.000 --> 00:31:27.000
was a useful vegetation, was a nice,

00:31:27.000 --> 00:31:29.000
pleasant vegetation.

00:31:33.000 --> 00:31:35.000
And furthermore,

00:31:35.000 --> 00:31:37.000
at that moment,

00:31:39.000 --> 00:31:41.000
God did not

00:31:41.000 --> 00:31:43.000
make rain to fall upon the earth.

00:31:44.000 --> 00:31:46.000
We're going to see how

00:31:46.000 --> 00:31:48.000
there was, you know,

00:31:48.000 --> 00:31:50.000
how the vegetation could grow

00:31:50.000 --> 00:31:52.000
if there was no tree, if there was no rain,

00:31:52.000 --> 00:31:54.000
but there was no rain yet.

00:31:58.000 --> 00:32:00.000
The Torah is describing

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:02.000
the world

00:32:02.000 --> 00:32:04.000
as it was prior

00:32:04.000 --> 00:32:06.000
to Adam's sin.

00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:08.000
And it says,

00:32:08.000 --> 00:32:10.000
all the bushes

00:32:10.000 --> 00:32:12.000
of the, you know,

00:32:12.000 --> 00:32:14.000
all the thorns, the vegetation

00:32:14.000 --> 00:32:16.000
in the field, telling me about

00:32:16.000 --> 00:32:18.000
how it was not in the land, didn't grow,

00:32:18.000 --> 00:32:20.000
because there was no weeds,

00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:22.000
telling his heart, his mind,

00:32:22.000 --> 00:32:24.000
it still did not grow.

00:32:24.000 --> 00:32:26.000
God also did not make rain

00:32:26.000 --> 00:32:28.000
to fall on the land.

00:32:28.000 --> 00:32:30.000
In other words, there was no rain.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:32.000
We're going to see

00:32:32.000 --> 00:32:34.000
how it was, the tree.

00:32:34.000 --> 00:32:36.000
Now we're there in heaven.

00:32:36.000 --> 00:32:38.000
No, the way it was

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:40.000
in earth.

00:32:40.000 --> 00:32:42.000
We're going to see it.

00:32:42.000 --> 00:32:44.000
In heaven we're going to see it.

00:32:44.000 --> 00:32:46.000
It's on earth. We're going to see

00:32:46.000 --> 00:32:48.000
what it means. But I need

00:32:48.000 --> 00:32:50.000
gentlemen.

00:32:50.000 --> 00:32:52.000
I'm overusing my voice.

00:32:56.000 --> 00:32:58.000
So be,

00:32:58.000 --> 00:33:00.000
try to be silent.

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:02.000
I don't have to overuse

00:33:02.000 --> 00:33:04.000
my voice.

00:33:04.000 --> 00:33:06.000
And

00:33:06.000 --> 00:33:08.000
there's a question here

00:33:08.000 --> 00:33:10.000
You're saying that he did not make rain.

00:33:10.000 --> 00:33:12.000
Yes, rain

00:33:12.000 --> 00:33:14.000
itself is a natural act.

00:33:14.000 --> 00:33:16.000
Maybe the phrase should be

00:33:16.000 --> 00:33:18.000
he did not lay down the law for the making of rain.

00:33:18.000 --> 00:33:20.000
Yes, yes.

00:33:20.000 --> 00:33:22.000
It's a natural act.

00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:24.000
And there was no human

00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:26.000
who had

00:33:26.000 --> 00:33:28.000
to plow the land.

00:33:28.000 --> 00:33:30.000
In other words, there was Adam,

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:32.000
but Adam did not have to plow the land

00:33:32.000 --> 00:33:34.000
to make the land grow.

00:33:34.000 --> 00:33:36.000
So how does it work, the world, in such a

00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:38.000
such a state? We're going to see.

00:33:38.000 --> 00:33:40.000
You know,

00:33:40.000 --> 00:33:42.000
underneath the earth

00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:44.000
there is water.

00:33:44.000 --> 00:33:46.000
And this is a fact.

00:33:46.000 --> 00:33:48.000
Underneath there is

00:33:48.000 --> 00:33:50.000
water.

00:33:50.000 --> 00:33:52.000
Now, in those days,

00:33:52.000 --> 00:33:54.000
the

00:33:54.000 --> 00:33:56.000
from the

00:33:56.000 --> 00:33:58.000
under the land,

00:33:58.000 --> 00:34:00.000
from under the earth,

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:02.000
from underneath would come

00:34:02.000 --> 00:34:04.000
water in the form of

00:34:04.000 --> 00:34:06.000
vapor.

00:34:06.000 --> 00:34:08.000
It would be like vapor coming from underneath the earth.

00:34:08.000 --> 00:34:10.000
And therefore, everything would be water.

00:34:10.000 --> 00:34:12.000
The vegetation would be water.

00:34:12.000 --> 00:34:14.000
Not from above,

00:34:14.000 --> 00:34:16.000
but from beneath.

00:34:16.000 --> 00:34:18.000
By the way, irrigation was from beneath.

00:34:18.000 --> 00:34:20.000
By the way,

00:34:20.000 --> 00:34:22.000
the Israelis are very astute.

00:34:22.000 --> 00:34:24.000
And they discovered, the Israelis,

00:34:24.000 --> 00:34:26.000
that the best way to irrigate

00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:28.000
the plant is not from above,

00:34:28.000 --> 00:34:30.000
but from beneath.

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:32.000
And now when they have a special system of irrigation

00:34:32.000 --> 00:34:34.000
because the water comes from beneath,

00:34:34.000 --> 00:34:36.000
it goes straight to the roots, you know,

00:34:36.000 --> 00:34:38.000
so there is no waste of water

00:34:38.000 --> 00:34:40.000
and it grows much better.

00:34:40.000 --> 00:34:42.000
You see? So in the same way

00:34:42.000 --> 00:34:44.000
as the Israelis, they do it now,

00:34:44.000 --> 00:34:46.000
this was

00:34:46.000 --> 00:34:48.000
the way that everything would be

00:34:48.000 --> 00:34:50.000
irrigated from

00:34:50.000 --> 00:34:52.000
